", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-94bb9c76894d7f65", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-94bb9c76894d7f65", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{"command": "cd /workspace/design && cat > DESIGN.md <<'EOF'\n# Design: extracting and analysing basic molecular properties from a chemical dataset\nSTATUS: DESIGN ONLY. Nothing here has been executed as an experiment. Every numeric value\nbelow is an EXPECTED value fixed in advance, produced by arithmetic on hand-written\nformulas, not an observation.\n\n## 1. Question and answer form\nQuestion: how can basic molecular properties be extracted and analysed from a chemical dataset?\nThe design answers it in three claims that a run could support or refute:\n  C1 EXTRACTION  a descriptor layer turns one-row-per-molecule SMILES into a tidy,\n     typed, complete property table with zero silent failures.\n  C2 CORRECTNESS extracted values agree with pre-fixed independent references (hard gate).\n  C3 ANALYSIS    the resulting table supports interpretable distributional, correlational\n     and subgroup statements, and those statements are auditable per molecule.\n\n## 2. Input schema (portable; this is the contract, not the data)\nOne row per molecule, tab-separated/CSV, header required:\n  id            str  unique, non-empty\n  name          str  optional display label\n  stratum       str  optional; used for subgroup contrasts ('druglike'|'extreme'|...)\n  smiles        str  REQUIRED, canonical or isomeric SMILES, one molecule per row\n  formula       str  optional; if present it becomes a cross-check, never a source of truth\n  <expected_*>  optional pre-declared reference columns for the control table only\nSwap rule: a public set (e.g. a ChEMBL/BDDS/PubChem subset export) is usable unchanged if it\ncan be projected onto {id, smiles} with smiles >=99% non-empty. All downstream code, controls\nand analyses are unchanged; only the reference-value columns must be regenerated, because\nexpected values are curated against THIS 47-molecule set.\nInstance used here: ./MOLECULES.csv -- 47 analyzable rows (20 druglike, 27 extreme/edge) +\n5 negative-control rows. Curated by hand, no external dataset was available or required.\n\n## 3. Descriptor layer (pinned)\nEnvironment contract: CPython >=3.9, rdkit >=2023.09 (record exact version+build at run\ntime; log it), pandas optional (CSV I/O only), numpy optional (correlations only). No GPU,\nno network. Record: python -c \"from rdkit import Chem; print(Chem.rdmol.__version__)\" and\nsha256 of the input CSV.\n\nProperty -> exact call (all from one Mol object, no re-parsing):\n  heavy_atoms      mol.GetNumHeavyAtoms()\n  MW_avg           Descriptors.MolWt\n  MW_monoisotopic  Descriptors.MolWt is NOT it; use Descriptors.ExactMolWt\n  formula          rdMolDescriptors.CalcMolFormula(mol)\n  formal_charge    Chem.GetFormalCharge(mol)              (net, over all fragments)\n  n_HBD            rdMolDescriptors.CalcNumHBD(mol)\n  n_HBA            rdMolDescriptors.CalcNumHBA(mol)\n  TPSA             rdMolDescriptors.CalcTPSA(mol)\n  n_rot            rdMolDescriptors.CalcNumRotatableBonds(mol)   # definition is\n                                            # VERSION-DEPENDENT: pin and report the version\n  n_rings          rdMolDescriptors.CalcNumRings(mol)\n  n_arom_rings     rdMolDescriptors.CalcNumAromaticRings(mol)\n  n_heteroatoms    rdMolDescriptors.CalcNumHeteroatoms(mol)\n  frac_CSP3        rdMolDescriptors.CalcFractionCSP3(mol)\n  clogP            Descriptors.MolLogP(mol)               # Crippen estimate, model output\nTwo definitions are pinned explicitly because they are known to differ between tools:\n  * HBA/HBD = RDKit Lipinski-style atom-count SMARTS. Other tools (e.g. CDK, Open Babel)\n    give different counts on amides/sulfonamides. Never mix toolkits inside one table.\n  * clogP is a GROUP-CONTRIBUTION ESTIMATE, not a measurement. It is reported as an\n    estimate and is never used as ground truth for a molecule's true lipophilicity.\nFailure contract (the load-bearing engineering decision):\n  mol = Chem.MolFromSmiles(smi)   -> returns None on parse failure.\n  If None, the pipeline MUST raise and record (id, reason). It must never emit a row of\n  zeros, never coerce None to NaN-by-default, and never continue a batch silently.\n  Rationale: a row of zeros is indistinguishable from a molecule with genuinely zero\n  HBD/HBA/TPSA, so the failure is invisible in every downstream statistic. NEG1-NEG5 exist\n  to keep this honest.\nCanonical round-trip: canonical = Chem.MolToSmiles(mol); re-parse and re-describe; the two\ndescriptor vectors must be bit-identical. This catches toolkit-level instability, not curation.\n\n## 4. Extraction-stage controls (pre-specified; EXPECTED values, not results)\nEach control = expected value + named failure mode. A control with no failure mode is theatre.\n  K1 MW hard gate (load-bearing). MW_avg from RDKit must match the hand-audited MW computed\n     here from molecular formulas and CIAAW-2021 abridged atomic weights (./shadow_check.py).\n     Tolerance |diff| <= 0.05 for CHNOPS-F-Cl-Br-I, <= 0.10 for B/Si (abridged-atomic-weight\n     interval). Expected pass: 47/47. Anchors: ethanol 46.069, aspirin 180.159,\n     caffeine 194.194, beta-D-glucopyranose 180.156, eicosane 282.556, iodoform 393.732.\n     Failure mode: wrong atomic-weight table, unit confusion (monoisotopic vs average),\n     or a parser dropping atoms/fragments. If K1 fails, STOP: nothing downstream is trusted.\n  K2 formula cross-check. CalcMolFormula(mol) must equal the curated formula column after\n     hill-system normalisation (salts: compare per-fragment multiset, since RDKit writes\n     e.g. C4H12N+ plus Cl- separately). Failure mode: implicit-H errors, lost counter-ions.\n  K3 heavy-atom agreement. GetNumHeavyAtoms == hand census == formula heavy count, 47/47.\n     This already ran at design time and FOUND TWO CURATION ERRORS (see 7.3): ibuprofen was\n     missing a CH2, cyclen was one CH2-CH2 short of the 12-membered ring.\n  K4 negative controls. All 5 must be REJECTED with a specific recorded reason:\n     NEG1 'not_a_smiles', NEG2 empty string, NEG3 'C1CC' unclosed ring,\n     NEG4 'C(=O)(=O)(=O)O' over-valent carbon, NEG5 'q1ccccc1' unknown element.\n     Expected: 5/5 rejected, 0 rows of zeros produced. Failure mode: try/except that\n     defaults to 0.0 -- the single most damaging bug in this class of pipeline.\n  K5 zero-is-legitimate control. Benzene (B01): n_HBD=0, n_HBA=0, TPSA=0, n_rot=0,\n     n_rings=1, n_arom_rings=1. Paired with K4 this is what makes K4 meaningful: 0 must be\n     achievable AND must not be the failure channel.\n  K6 charge handling. B17 choline cation net +1; B18 acetate -1; B20 methyl hydrogen\n     sulfate -1; B19 glycine zwitterion and B16 tetramethylammonium chloride and B21\n     nitrobenzene net 0 but with formal charges present internally (GetFormalCharge alone\n     returns 0 -- so n_charged_atoms must be recorded alongside net charge, or charged\n     molecules are mislabelled neutral). Failure mode: charge stripped during\n     sanitisation/cleaning, silently converting an ion into a neutral radical-like species.\n  K7 tautomer sensitivity, stated as a limitation probe. B22 2-hydroxypyridine and\n     B23 2-pyridone are the SAME formula (C5H5NO) and identical MW, but as written give\n     different HBD/HBA/TPSA (expected 1/2 vs 2/1). No tautomer standardisation is applied.\n     Expected: values DIFFER. If they come out identical the descriptor layer is not doing\n     what we think; if they differ, the analysis in 5 must not treat tautomers as\n     independent chemistry without a caveat.\n  K8 stereo-insensitivity. B24 L-alanine vs B25 D-alanine: every achiral descriptor in the\n     table must be identical. Failure mode: a fingerprint/descriptor that accidentally\n     encodes chirality, breaking subgroup pooling.\n  K9 version reproducibility. Re-run the whole table on a second pinned RDKit minor\n     version. Expected: MW/formula/heavy/charge/HBD/HBA/TPSA/rings identical; n_rot and\n     frac_CSP3 flagged for explicit diff because their definitions have changed across\n     releases. Any disagreement in the first group = environment problem, not a finding.\n  K10 round-trip idempotence. canonical SMILES -> re-parse -> descriptors must equal the\n     originals for 47/47 molecules.\n\nDisconfirming evidence (what would make us abandon the pipeline, pre-declared):\n  * any single molecule where MW_avg disagrees with the formula arithmetic by > tolerance\n    AND the disagreement survives re-parsing from a hand-written molblock -> the toolkit\n    usage is wrong or the input column is not SMILES;\n  * any negative control that yields a value instead of an error -> the failure contract is\n    broken and EVERY downstream summary is suspect, because silent zeros bias all means;\n  * benzene reporting TPSA > 0 or an aromatic ring count of 0 -> aromaticity perception is\n    off, which invalidates n_arom_rings, logP and TPSA across the whole set;\n  * round-trip descriptors differing for >=1 molecule -> the table is not reproducible and\n    cannot be shared;\n  * L/D-alanine differing on any achiral descriptor -> atom-ordering or sanitisation\n    nondeterminism, i.e. the extraction is not a function of the molecule.\n\n## 5. Analysis plan (on the extracted table; all thresholds fixed before running)\nNotation: n=47 analyzable, 2 strata (20/27). Everything below is descriptive at this n.\n  A1 Distributions: median [IQR], min, max, and 5-95 percentile for MW, clogP, TPSA,\n     n_rot; integer counts shown as frequency tables. Skew-prone columns (MW, TPSA, clogP)\n     also shown log-scaled so that the shape statement is not scale-dependent.\n     Reference arithmetic for the MW column (from ./shadow_check.py, EXPECTED):\n     n=47 min 46.07, max 393.73, median 132.12, mean 154.92, sd 81.39. Any run whose MW\n     summary drifts materially from this has an extraction problem, not a chemistry story.\n     NOTE: this set is deliberately NOT a random chemical sample. Its distribution describes\n     the curation, not any population. No inference to a wider population is made.\n  A2 Redundancy / collinearity (kept because it is a genuine control on interpretation):\n     Spearman matrix over MW, heavy_atoms, TPSA, n_HBD+n_HBA, n_rot, frac_CSP3.\n     EXPECTED: MW~heavy_atoms rho >= 0.95 (arithmetic, not discovery), TPSA~(HBD+HBA) high,\n     MW~TPSA high. Pre-declared consequence: these are collinear by construction, so they\n     are presented as ONE size/polarity axis and never as independent risk factors; a\n     rule-of-five style screen is reported as a sum of parts, not four findings.\n     Sanity use: if MW~heavy_atoms came out near 0, the descriptor columns would be\n     misaligned across rows -- a data-join bug this test catches cheaply.\n  A3 Subgroup contrasts, DESCRIPTIVE ONLY (scope guard honoured):\n     strata = druglike vs extreme; charged (|charge|>0 or internal charges) vs neutral;\n     HBD=0 vs HBD>0; n_arom_rings=0 vs >=1.\n     Reported per contrast: group medians and IQRs, Hodges-Lehmann shift estimate,\n     rank-biserial effect size, and the fraction of overlap between groups. Mann-Whitney U\n     is computed but PRE-DECLARED LOW-POWERED AND NON-REPORTABLE AS A FINDING at n~20/27;\n     it is included only for reproducibility, and no p-value is quoted as a result anywhere.\n     EXPECTED directions (sanity, not discovery): extreme stratum higher n_rot and lower\n     TPSA-normalised polarity; charged set higher TPSA; these are near-tautological given\n     curation and are labelled as such.\n  A4 Rule-based screen: Lipinski (MW>500, clogP>5, HBD>5, HBA>10) and rule-of-three\n     (MW<300, clogP<3) violation counts per molecule with the violating descriptor named.\n     EXPECTED: a