{"id": "ba94579e-db0d-4002-b5fb-5ff169ce537a", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:23:22.979261", "source": "user", "parent_id": "03b227a8-d144-4388-b983-6cf5f7820f73", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "This is a strong, honest deliverable and most of it I would accept as-is. The three refutable claims, the pinned descriptor layer, the zero-suppression failure contract, the demotion of Mann–Whitney to non-reportable, the recalled-logP quarantine, and especially the self-inflicted-bug log in §8.3 are exactly what a defensible design looks like. The dry-run sabotage tests are the right way to show controls are live rather than decorative: the +3 Da / +0.02 Da pair demonstrates the K1 tolerance has real discriminating power, and the K4 result under a swallowed-exception mock demonstrates the failure mode the design exists to catch. Nothing here reads as a fabricated result, and the expected-vs-obtained labeling holds.\n\nOne identifiable mismatch to close, then I want the final candidate.\n\n**The Lipinski/rule-of-five axis is planned but unexercised, and that leaves a vacuous step in the analysis plan.** Your own consistency audit caught it: max MW 393.73 < 500 across the whole curated set, so no molecule can produce a MW violation, and by the same logic the HBD>5 / HBA>10 / logP>5 axes are likely equally inert. We agreed on a rule-of-five/three screen as an interpretable summary; as instantiated, that step cannot return anything but \"zero violations for all 47\", which is a null produced by curation rather than a finding about the data. Pick one of these and make it explicit in DESIGN.md — do not leave it silently both ways:\n\n(a) *Close it cheaply.* Add a small third stratum (3–5 molecules, no execution needed) that crosses the boundaries: something >500 Da, something with >10 HBA, something with >10 rotatable bonds, ideally one genuine multi-violator. This makes the screen non-vacuous and 